WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.664
npm security is software supply chain
security applied to everyday JavaScript and

2
00:00:07.744 --> 00:00:08.972
Node.js work.

3
00:00:09.052 --> 00:00:14.375
The package manager gives teams fast
access to reusable code,

4
00:00:14.455 --> 00:00:20.084
but it also brings transitive
dependencies, registry behavior,

5
00:00:20.164 --> 00:00:25.076
install-time execution, account
permissions, tokens,

6
00:00:25.156 --> 00:00:29.967
and release workflows into the application
risk model.

7
00:00:30.047 --> 00:00:36.187
Secure teams do not treat package
installation as a background detail.

8
00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:40.668
They treat it as a controlled engineering
process.

9
00:00:40.748 --> 00:00:45.763
A practical checklist starts with
dependency selection.

10
00:00:45.843 --> 00:00:53.110
Install packages intentionally, review
package purpose and maintenance signals,

11
00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:58.922
and prefer smaller dependency footprints
when they meet the need.

12
00:00:59.002 --> 00:01:04.938
Use package.json to understand declared
dependencies and scripts.

13
00:01:05.018 --> 00:01:09.521
Use package-lock.json to review the
resolved tree.

14
00:01:09.601 --> 00:01:16.458
Use npm ci in automation so CI proves the
committed lockfile instead of silently

15
00:01:16.538 --> 00:01:19.916
resolving new versions during a build.

16
00:01:19.996 --> 00:01:22.759
Then add operational controls.

17
00:01:22.839 --> 00:01:28.366
Triage audit findings with context, test
remediation changes,

18
00:01:28.446 --> 00:01:31.312
and avoid blind automated fixes.

19
00:01:31.392 --> 00:01:38.556
Pay attention to lifecycle scripts and
minimize what secrets are available during

20
00:01:38.636 --> 00:01:39.455
install.

21
00:01:39.535 --> 00:01:46.494
Configure scopes and registries so
internal packages resolve from the intended

22
00:01:46.574 --> 00:01:47.291
source.

23
00:01:47.371 --> 00:01:51.874
Protect npm accounts, use least-privilege
tokens,

24
00:01:51.954 --> 00:01:59.323
and prefer publishing workflows that
produce provenance evidence where available.

25
00:01:59.403 --> 00:02:02.985
Finally, make the practice sustainable.

26
00:02:03.065 --> 00:02:10.229
Route dependency changes through review,
document exceptions, track ownership,

27
00:02:10.309 --> 00:02:13.585
and keep CI policies understandable.

28
00:02:13.665 --> 00:02:19.396
The best npm security program is not the
one with the most warnings.

29
00:02:19.476 --> 00:02:25.617
It is the one where developers can explain
what changed, why it changed,

30
00:02:25.697 --> 00:02:31.940
how it was verified, and what risk remains
before the code reaches users.
